A **mobile-first** media query writes the small-screen layout as the unqualified default, then layers on complexity with `min-width` queries as the viewport grows:

```css
.card { display: block; }
@media (min-width: 768px) { .card { display: flex; } }
```

A **desktop-first** approach does the reverse: the default is the full desktop layout, and `max-width` queries strip things down for smaller screens.

Mobile-first is the industry default for a concrete reason, not just convention: small-screen CSS tends to be simpler (single column, stacked), so writing it as the base case means less CSS ships to every device, and mobile devices — often on weaker connections — never have to download and immediately override desktop-only rules. Desktop-first commonly ends up shipping unused desktop styles to every phone, then fighting them with overrides.

A media query only ever knows the viewport's width — it has no idea that a component is sitting inside a 300px sidebar on a 1920px-wide screen. That mismatch is why a card component styled purely with media queries often looks fine in a full-width layout and cramped the moment a designer drops it into a narrower sidebar.

Container queries fix this by querying the size of a **containing element**, not the viewport:

```css
.card-wrapper {
  container-type: inline-size;
  container-name: card;
}
@container card (min-width: 400px) {
  .card { display: flex; }
}
```

The component now genuinely doesn't care where it's placed — it responds to the box it's actually given, which is what makes it a real building block for a component library rather than something that only looks right at the one placement it was designed for.

Before `clamp()`, fluid type meant choosing between a fixed size (doesn't scale) or a pure `vw` size (scales but has no floor or ceiling, so it gets unreadably small or absurdly huge at the extremes). `clamp(min, preferred, max)` gives both bounds in one line:

```css
h1 {
  font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw + 1rem, 3.5rem);
}
```

Read it as: never smaller than `1.75rem`, never larger than `3.5rem`, and in between, scale fluidly with viewport width via the middle expression. The `+ 1rem` in the preferred value also keeps it partially tied to `rem`, so it still respects user zoom rather than being purely viewport-driven.

The same pattern works for `width`, `padding`, or any numeric property — `clamp()` is the general fix for "I want this to scale, but not without limits," and it replaces most of what used to require a media query per breakpoint just to adjust one size.
